WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.660
It can be tough to figure out what to stream
next in Netflix's vast library, but don't

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:05.160
worry.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.600
From incredible underrated gems to timeless
favorites that you haven't thought about in

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.800
years, we've rounded up the best movies on
Netflix right now.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:16.540
After The Amazing Spider-Man 2 effectively
killed a franchise as we knew it, it came

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:19.981
as a bit of a shock when Sony Pictures announced
that they'd be moving forward with their own

00:00:19.981 --> 00:00:23.980
new Spider-Man project after agreeing sharing
the web-slinger with Marvel Studios.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.480
That task seemed all the more daunting when
Spider-Man: Homecoming arrived like the John

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.540
Hughes Spider-flick we didn't know we needed.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.600
Then came Sony's animated Spider-Man: Into
the Spider-Verse, which proved that the movie

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:37.770
multi-verse was big enough for more than one
Spider-Man not to mention a Spider-Gwen, a

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.220
Spider-Ham, and a giant anime robot from the
future.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:44.559
Into the Spider-Verse breathed a welcome life
into both the rapidly tiring superhero genre

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.790
and the stagnating medium of feature animation.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.200
That life arrived via Miles Morales, who seeks
to replace the recently deceased Spider-Man

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.079
of his world, only to discover that there
are several Spider-Folk spread across multiple

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.880
dimensions, and that a singular threat is
about to disrupt each of their realities.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:03.660
The story, chock full of equal parts drama,
comedy, and heartfelt soul, unfolds in an

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:07.360
electrifying visual style designed to literally
bring comic book pages to life.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.680
That hearty combination of style and substance
makes Into the Spider-Verse unlike any other

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:12.920
superhero flick out there.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:18.499
It's easy to forget, but prior to 2005's Batman
Begins, there wasn't a ton of support behind

00:01:18.499 --> 00:01:19.770
making a new Batman flick.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:23.880
That had a lot to do with the sour taste Joel
Schumacher's shamelessly indulgent camp-fest

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.780
Batman and Robin left in the mouths of Bat-fans
the world over.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.600
So when a then-relatively-unknown Christopher
Nolan signed up to bring the Dark Knight's

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.660
big screen saga back to its former glory,
it definitely raised a few eyebrows.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:39.259
When Batman Begins finally hit theaters in
the summer of '05, those eyebrows went from

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.889
warily raised to wide-eyed with glee almost
overnight.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:46.380
Rather than the neon-drenched visuals and
over-the-top puns, this was the Batman film

00:01:46.389 --> 00:01:50.829
so many fans had been waiting for a gritty,
action-packed character drama about a troubled

00:01:50.829 --> 00:01:54.929
young man embracing his inner heart of darkness
in the wake of witnessing his parent's brutal

00:01:54.929 --> 00:01:55.420
murder.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:59.700
The fact that said man was Bruce Wayne, played
with equal parts charm and pathos by Christian

00:01:59.709 --> 00:02:03.659
Bale, and that his heart of darkness led him
to become the vigilante known as Batman, only

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.120
made the story more intriguing.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.890
While Nolan and company packed in plenty of
the white-knuckle action one would expect

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:13.120
in any Caped Crusader tale throughout Batman
Begins, the film had an incredible passion

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.300
for character, particularly Michael Caine's
pitch-perfect Alfred.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.700
"Sir, There's a problem with the graphic sir.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.180
The next 10,000 will be up to our specifications.“

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:25.540
“At least they gave us a discount."

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:30.480
Ultimately, that led Nolan's Batflick to do
something no other Batman movie had before

00:02:30.489 --> 00:02:32.879
resonate with viewers on a deeply human level.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:38.530
In 2010, graphic novel fans were excited for
Scott Pilgrim vs. The World, and not just

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.280
because they liked the source material.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.180
The news that the film was to be directed
by Edgar Wright then a burgeoning cult icon

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:48.230
hot off the success of Shaun of the Dead and
Hot Fuzz left fans downright impatient for

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:49.580
it to arrive in theaters.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:54.159
Wright delivered the goods, crafting an action-packed,
genre-busting romantic comedy with enough

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.760
razor-sharp wit and eight-bit charm to claim
success.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.900
It also featured a hip young cast with scene-stealing
appearances from future MCU stars Chris Evans

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:02.920
and Brie Larson.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:07.060
With that pedigree, and reviews to match,
Scott Pilgrim vs. The World seemed destined

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:10.040
for box office glory, but sadly, that didn't
happen.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:14.320
In fact, the film was an outright bomb that
seemed doomed to claim little more than "cult

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:14.940
classic" status.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.100
Luckily, Wright's hipster opus found second
life on video, and is now counted amongst

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:20.320
the director's best work.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:23.160
If you've been sleeping on that fact, now's
the time to get into the game.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:27.019
Horror fans are currently enjoying a golden
age of scary cinema.

00:03:27.019 --> 00:03:31.159
It seems like every single week, a smart,
scary confection is hitting theaters to rave

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:32.960
reviews and box office conquest.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:36.940
Sadly, that means that every year, there's
a buried treasure that goes unnoticed, much

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.740
like a body stashed in a shallow grave.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:43.120
One of the more overlooked gems of late has
to be the 2016 pseudo-haunted house chiller

00:03:43.129 --> 00:03:46.520
The Autopsy of Jane Doe, from director André
Øvredal.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:51.290
If you recognize Øvredal's name, it's likely
because you saw his 2010 found-footage masterpiece

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:52.080
Trollhunter.

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:55.840
If you're wondering why you never heard about
his follow-up, well, the film received virtually

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.170
no promotion from distributors en route to
its theatrical release and was all but ignored

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:01.540
by audiences in theaters.

00:04:01.709 --> 00:04:06.520
Luckily, Øvredal's atmospheric creeper has
garnered legit cult-classic status via streaming

00:04:06.529 --> 00:04:10.010
platforms, so if you've yet to experience
the morbid delights within The Autopsy of

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:12.160
Jane Doe, there's no time like the present.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:16.500
Without spoiling anything, the film is part
haunting procedural mystery and part gory,

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:19.750
ghoulish fright fest, centered around the
gruesome act of the title.

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:24.130
Øvredal makes marvelous use of the film's
grim, single location, a morgue, and nothing

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:27.060
can quite prepare you for the grisly mystery
unfolding within.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:33.140
First published in 2004, the epic, mind-bending
sci-fi novel Cloud Atlas was hailed in some

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:38.470
literary circles as a visionary work of speculative
fiction, which, unsurprisingly made many readers

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:41.500
believe that it would be totally impossible
to adapt to the big screen.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:46.580
Clearly, nobody told Tom Tykwer or the Wachowskis
that fact, and the adventurous trio set out

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:50.320
to bring their sprawling, star-studded, and
unabashedly ambitious adaptation of Cloud

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:54.040
Atlas to the world a few years later only
to see the world pretty much ignore it.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:55.360
That was the world's loss.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.900
Set over six different time periods, the film
features a brilliant ensemble cast fronted

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:04.520
by Tom Hanks, Halle Berry, Hugh Grant, and
Susan Sarandon, all playing multiple roles

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:05.020
throughout.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:09.620
The loosely intertwined narratives start and
stop with little notice, making Cloud Atlas

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:14.130
an admittedly challenging slice of experimental
cinema for the blockbuster audience it targeted.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:18.030
And if those elements weren't enough to scare
that audience off, the film's almost three-hour

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:20.340
runtime was probably enough to seal the deal.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:21.860
"I think you're gonna love this."

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:33.240
Those brave enough to buy the ticket and take
the supremely wild ride were rewarded with

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:38.220
a visionary work of cinematic daring so full
of visual thrills, stark human drama, and

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:42.820
complex philosophical ideologies that it could
spin the head of even the savviest of audiences.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:47.599
To those who missed out, we'd wager this marvelous
head-trip of a film will be a bit more digestible

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:48.900
in the comforts of your own home.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:54.380
In the summer of 2007, a teaser that ran for
just under two minutes alongside the brand

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:58.430
new Transformers film made moviegoers way
more intrigued than any of Michael Bay's robotic

00:05:58.430 --> 00:05:59.360
trucks ever did.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:03.740
The unlabeled, inexplicable monster movie
ad from J.J. Abrams and the Bad Robot team

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:07.940
had fans buzzing for months to find out the
name of the film it teased, with a groundbreaking

00:06:07.949 --> 00:06:12.020
viral marketing campaign that ensured they
would stay hooked until the movie was released..

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:16.400
By the time Cloverfield made its premiere
the next January, the fans were beyond fever-pitch.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:21.020
Against all odds, Cloverfield directed with
fevered urgency by Matt Reeves and bolstered

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:25.840
by Drew Goddard's agile scriptingdidn't just
meet its lofty expectations, it far surpassed

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:26.340
them.

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:30.260
Reeves and Godard delivered the humane, grounded
monster-movie masterpiece a generation of

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:34.159
filmgoers never knew they needed, using a
found-footage style to give the audience a

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:36.820
first-person view of the city-stomping kaiju
 action.

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:41.600
Over a decade later, Cloverfield still packs
the same visceral punch that it did upon release,

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:46.020
and remains a fascinating entry point to the
cinematic world-building experiment that led

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:50.139
to the underrated 10 Cloverfield Lane and
the compelling disaster that was The Cloverfield

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:54.980
Paradox — which, like Cloverfield itself,
are far more rewarding with repeat viewings.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:07:00.419
In the years since he broke onto the international
film scene in the early 2000s, South Korean

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:04.380
auteur Bong Joon-ho has become one of the
most respected filmmakers working today.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:08.780
That's mostly thanks to one of the most adventurous
filmographies cinema has ever seen.

00:07:08.789 --> 00:07:13.139
His string of surprises just kept going in
2013 when he adapted a little-known graphic

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:16.280
novel called Snowpiercer as his English-language
debut.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.620
Though many foreign filmmakers struggle in
adapting their style to American markets,

00:07:20.629 --> 00:07:24.449
Bong took the challenge in stride, and delivered
one of the more bracingly original sci-fi

00:07:24.449 --> 00:07:25.800
films of the modern era.

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:29.949
Snowpiercer unfolds on a speeding train that's
spent 17 straight years cutting through a

00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:34.860
desolate landscape decimated by a failed climate
change experiment, and even amongst this director's

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:37.200
eclectic filmography, it's one hell of a wild
ride.

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:42.100
As with much of Bong's work, it's also politically
charged, with climate science in play outside

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:44.560
the train and class warfare broiling inside.

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:48.510
We wouldn't dare spoil one wild minute of
this film for those who haven't seen it, but

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:52.500
with an incredible performance by Chris Evans
that gets pretty far from Captain America,

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:54.430
things get crazy in ways you cannot fathom.

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:55.840
And that's a very good thing.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:01.240
In the 35 years since Joe Dante's Gremlins
hit theaters, there's been an ongoing debate

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:02.760
about how the film should be classified.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:05.940
Some think it's a classic black comedy full
of slapstick violence.

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:10.400
Others think it's a gory horror flick, while
others think it's a treatise against the commercialization

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:11.400
of the holiday season.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:15.639
There are even folks who think it's the greatest
Christmas movie ever made, for a certain definition

00:08:15.639 --> 00:08:16.930
of "Christmas movie."

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:19.919
The only thing you really need to understand
about Gremlins is that Gremlins doesn't give

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:21.200
a hoot how you classify it.

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:23.260
So yes, Gremlins is a dark comedy.

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:25.220
Yes, it's an anti-commercial Christmas movie.

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:29.160
And yes, it's a classic slice of creature
feature horror to boot, delivering one of

00:08:29.169 --> 00:08:33.620
the cutest creatures to ever grace the silver
screen in Gizmo, and ingeniously ensuring

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:37.790
that the cuter-than-cute creature inadvertently
spawns the murderously grotesque monsters

00:08:37.790 --> 00:08:38.900
that give the film its name.

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:42.120
Look, we aren't trying to tell you how to
watch Gremlins, as long as you actually watch

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:42.620
it.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:45.520
Just know that whether you're seeing it for
the first time or the twentieth, it's that

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:50.820
rarest of creepy, socially conscious, creature
feature comedies that delivers a few new surprises

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:51.880
with every single watch.

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:56.560
One of Michael Mann's earliest claims to fame
came when he leveraged his penchant for tough

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:00.930
guy crime tales and his edgy sense of cool
to maximum effect as a key creative force

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:03.080
behind smash hit TV series Miami Vice.

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:07.840
In the years since that show ended in 1990,
Mann has built a reputation as a visionary

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:12.930
cinematic stylist who Martin Scorsese once
called "One of the finest filmmakers in America."

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:16.870
Still, it was a bit of a surprise that, a
quarter century after the show was canceled,

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:20.180
Mann signed on to write and direct a big-screen
adaptation of the series.

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:25.140
To the surprise of exactly no one, Mann delivered
a slick neo-noir thriller steeped in the suave

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:29.560
machismo that made the series a hit, though
he did so sans the "buddy cop" camp that helped

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:31.040
soften the edges of the series.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:35.140
Make no mistake, Miami Vice — driven by
a pair of unflappably stoic performances from

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:38.840
Jamie Foxx and Colin Farrell — is an almost
absurdly serious film.

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:42.371
If you're willing to buy into that super-serious
tone, though, you're likely to see it for

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:47.800
exactly what it is: a subtly subversive, razor-sharp
crime drama with style and energy to burn,

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:52.000
all of which makes it a pitch-perfect entry
into Mann's already incredible filmography.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:56.940
Of all the noteworthy filmmakers who broke
through in the '90s, few did so with quite

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:58.510
as much verve as Guy Ritchie.

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:02.140
The British director made the scene with stylishly
kinetic and laugh-out-loud hilarious caper

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:05.200
comedies and delivered what might be his best
work in 2000.

00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:09.500
Titled simply Snatch, that film saw Ritchie
building on the styles and themes that made

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:13.700
his first hit, Lock, Stock, and Two Smoking
Barrels such a refreshing cinematic endeavor.

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:17.900
The breakneck cutting, outlandish violence,
and zinging dialogue from low-level criminal

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:22.660
losers bungling towards a big score delivered
a devilishly over-the-top crime adventure.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:26.660
It's a classic crime story complete with a
McGuffin, as the entirety of London's criminal

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:30.420
underground, from bare knuckle boxing promoters
to Russian gangsters, go on the hunt for a

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:32.010
priceless stolen diamond.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:34.780
"Ver is tha stoo-one?"

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:41.680
"Where is the stone?"

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:46.020
In service of bringing this marvelously madcap
diamond dash to life, Ritchie enlisted an

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:51.580
impressive A-list cast of U.S. and U.K. players,
including Benicio del Toro and a then relatively

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:55.720
unknown Jason Statham, along with tough-guy
footballer turned actor Vinnie Jones and,

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:57.900
of course, the big-name star, Brad Pitt.

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:01.140
"Hey mam come look at the size of this fella!

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:04.580
Bet you box a little, that’s for sure.

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:06.400
You look like a boxer!"

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:11.260
Pitt's involvement drew American audiences
to the theater, and as solid as the cast is

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:16.390
top to bottom, his miraculous turn as a shifty,
tough-as-nails, nearly incomprehensible boxer

00:11:16.390 --> 00:11:21.190
absolutely steals the show, and helps transform
Ritchie's clever genre retread into a crackerjack

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:22.690
crime comedy for the ages.

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:27.740
Today, Jennifer Lawrence is one of the most
beloved and recognizable stars in Hollywood,

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:31.460
but prior to 2010, you would've been hard
pressed to find anyone at all who knew her

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:31.960
name.

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:36.120
If you're at all familiar with Lawrence's
meteoric rise to legit superstardom, you know

00:11:36.130 --> 00:11:39.850
the ascension began when she landed the lead
in the gritty micro-budget drama Winter's

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:40.340
Bone.

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:43.640
As good as she's been in much of her work
since, Lawrence's performance in Winter's

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:44.940
Bone remains her best.

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:48.240
Her raw talent is full on display throughout
this diamond of a film.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:52.400
And we can thank director Debra Granik for
that, who cast Lawrence against type as a

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:56.520
dirt poor, tough-as-nails Ozark teen desperate
to find her missing, meth-dealing father.

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:00.760
In order to find the missing man, she's forced
to dive headlong down a rabbit hole full of

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:04.860
nefarious, backwoods-dwelling characters who
value their community's strict code of silence

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:05.480
above all else.

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:09.810
Chief among them is her enigmatic, addicted
Uncle Teardrop, played by John Hawkes in one

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:14.060
of the best supporting performances ever committed
to film, who's harboring some serious ill

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:15.580
will toward his missing brother.

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:17.960
"You always have scared me."

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:21.880
"That’s because you're smart."

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:26.200
To say any more would be to take the sting
right out of this brutal, beautifully brooding

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:30.700
little drama, and this is one mystery whose
secrets are worth discovering, and rediscovering,

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:31.200
for yourself.

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:36.440
Set in a vividly realized, not-too-distant
future, Her follows a kind, lonely man who

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:41.000
finds love in a most unexpected place with
an AI-driven operating system named Samantha.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:45.560
On paper, that plot sounds a bit ridiculous,
and in the hands of a lesser filmmaker, it

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:46.380
probably would've been.

00:12:46.730 --> 00:12:51.420
With Spike Jonze at the helm, Her's potentially
silly setup becomes a tenderly observed study

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:55.940
of longing for and finding connection in a
tech-obsessed world driven to isolationism.

00:12:56.280 --> 00:13:00.260
At the center of that world is a transcendent
performance from Joaquin Phoenix, who brings

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:02.600
a warmth, wit, and compassion to this role.

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:07.070
The supporting roles are fantastic, too, with
a scene-stealing turn from Amy Adams and dynamic

00:13:07.070 --> 00:13:11.290
voice work from Scarlett Johansson, who makes
a fully developed character of her artificial

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:11.780
persona.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:16.720
As a sci-fi film, Her is a towering, deeply
personal achievement in style and substance.

00:13:16.940 --> 00:13:20.630
As a social document, it's a visionary reflection
on the world as it likely will be.

00:13:20.630 --> 00:13:23.100
If you don't believe that, just ask Alexa
what she thinks.

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:28.420
Since breaking into Hollywood with 1984's
remarkably assured debut Blood Simple, the

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:33.080
Coen brothers, Joel and Ethan, have gone on
to produce a near-unimpeachable body of work.

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:37.410
Their films have found them working well in
virtually every genre, from slapstick farce

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:41.540
to romantic comedy, to elaborate crime stories,
and even the occasional musical numbers.

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:45.440
Whatever story they choose to tell, you can
be certain of one thing: the Coens are always

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:47.620
going to push to do something they haven't
done before.

00:13:47.770 --> 00:13:51.980
Though it features all the staples fans have
seen before, their 2018 Western The Ballad

00:13:51.980 --> 00:13:55.540
of Buster Scruggs finds the Coens pushing
themselves farther than ever.

00:13:59.380 --> 00:13:59.940
"First time?"

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:06.260
Of course, the thing that most obviously sets
The Ballad of Buster Scruggs apart from the

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:10.740
rest of their films is that it's an anthology
comprised of six short tales of the wild west

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:12.600
rather than a single, straightforward narrative.

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:17.110
Those six tales are thrilling, and hilarious,
and frequently downright heartbreaking, and

00:14:17.110 --> 00:14:21.500
feature stellar work from an all-star cast
including James Franco, Liam Neeson, and Tom

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:22.000
Waits.

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:26.700
Each one is fueled by the sort of mirthful
madness only Joel and Ethan Coen can conjure,

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:31.410
coupled with jaw-dropping digital cinematography
and pitch-perfect Western themes from composer

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:32.320
Carter Burwell.

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:36.880
The Ballad of Buster Scruggs is easily one
of the best movies Netflix has ever produced,

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:39.940
and has a good claim on being one of 2018's
best films.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:45.120
2018 may or may not have brought an official
end to Robert Redford's nearly six-decade

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:49.120
acting career, but retirement should offer
him a little more time in the director's seat.

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:52.750
That's a good thing, because Redford's work
behind the camera has often been as thrilling

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:55.640
as his work in front, even earning him an
Academy Award in 1981.

00:14:55.640 --> 00:15:00.680
In the years since winning that Oscar, Redford
has remained choosy with his directorial efforts,

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:02.520
helming only nine movies since.

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:06.400
Of those films, 1994's Quiz Show is arguably
his best work.

00:15:06.740 --> 00:15:10.680
Set in the '50s, it tells the true story of
one of television's most famous scandals.

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:14.680
The film follows a working-class man and a
member of one of America's most prominent

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:19.420
families as they face off on the hit TV quiz
show Twenty-One, which was infamously and

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:23.810
illegally rigged by the show's producers,
and eventually exposed by an idealistic government

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:24.300
attorney.

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:27.780
That may not sound like the blueprint for
a thrilling, character-driven biographical

00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:32.040
drama about corruption and classism in America,
but that's just what Redford delivered.

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:36.200
Like the story it so elegantly explores, Quiz
Show is almost too intriguing to believe,

00:15:36.200 --> 00:15:38.480
and remains one of the best films of the '90s.

